The program is small — read an [38;5;10mint[0m with [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m, then print its four storage bytes as lowercase hex plus a newline — so the translation work was mostly in reproducing C's input semantics faithfully:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mscanf[0m failure leaves [38;5;10mx[0m at its initial [38;5;10m0[0m, so empty input, [38;5;10m"abc"[0m, [38;5;10m"-"[0m, and [38;5;10m".5"[0m all print [38;5;10m00000000[0m. The Rust version mirrors this by ignoring the parse result rather than defaulting or erroring.[0m[0m
- Whitespace skipping crosses newlines, and parsing stops at the first non-digit, which is pushed back like [38;5;10mungetc[0m. I read stdin byte-at-a-time with one byte of lookahead instead of using line-based reads.[0m[0m
- glibc routes [38;5;10m%d[0m through [38;5;10mstrtol[0m, so the value saturates at [38;5;10mlong[0m range and is [3mthen[23m narrowed to [38;5;10mint[0m. That two-stage behavior is what makes [38;5;10m4294967296[0m print [38;5;10m00000000[0m and [38;5;10m99999999999999999999999[0m print [38;5;10mffffffff[0m; a single-step clamp to [38;5;10mi32[0m would get both wrong.[0m[0m
- Byte dumping uses [38;5;10mto_ne_bytes()[0m to keep the host's endianness, matching the C pointer cast.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One portability note: like the C, output is endianness- and [38;5;10msizeof(int)[0m-dependent, so it is identical on x86-64 Linux but would differ on a big-endian target.[0m[0m
